---
// The footer's install row — the journal's only advertisement for itself.
//
// WHERE IT SITS (editors, 2026-09-07). It is the third line of the footer's
// closing group, under "Subscribe to the journal" and "Support the journal" —
// three ways to keep reading us, matched. It stood higher up the stack until
// then, where it had taken the place of "No tracking, unsubscribe anytime."
// after the editors retired that line on 2026-09-02.
//
// THE ROW'S ONLY LINK, deliberately. Every other row in this footer is a list
// of destinations separated by dots. This one is a single thing to press, which
// is what keeps it legible as an offer rather than as one more address.
//
// THE MINI IS THE SERVED FAVICON, not a second drawing of the mark. Whatever
// public/favicon.svg holds is what the tab shows and what a home screen gets,
// so a reader who presses this row has already seen the icon they are about to
// install. One source; see assets/app-icon.svg.

import { APP_PAGE_PATH, APP_ROW_LABEL } from '../lib/app-icon.mjs';
---

<p class="footer-app">
  <a href={APP_PAGE_PATH}>
    <img class="footer-app-icon" src="/favicon.svg" width="16" height="16" alt="" aria-hidden="true" />
    <span>{APP_ROW_LABEL}</span>
  </a>
</p>

<style>
  /*
    HIDDEN WHEN THE JOURNAL IS ALREADY INSTALLED. An installed copy reports
    display-mode: standalone, so the offer to install disappears for exactly
    the readers who have taken it.

    CSS RATHER THAN SCRIPT, AND THE TRADE IS STATED. `navigator.standalone` is
    the older iOS signal and would catch iOS below 16.4, which this query does
    not; it would also mean a new inline script on every page of the site,
    during an open CSP observation window whose whole purpose is to get the
    inline scripts down to a set that can be nonced. One media query costs
    nothing and reports nothing. The readers it misses are those running an
    iOS from before March 2023 who have already installed the journal, and what
    they see is a row inviting them to do what they have done.

    THE FULLSCREEN AND MINIMAL-UI CASES are here because a launcher may honour
    a display mode the manifest did not ask for; the row should vanish whenever
    the journal is running as an app, not only in the one mode we requested.
  */
  @media (display-mode: standalone), (display-mode: fullscreen), (display-mode: minimal-ui) {
    .footer-app {
      display: none;
    }
  }

  /* THE ROW TAKES THE GROUP'S LETTERING RATHER THAN ITS OWN (editors,
     2026-09-07). Size, weight, letterspacing, case and colour are declared once
     on .footer-actions in Base.astro and inherit into this component; the
     declarations that used to sit here are deleted rather than restated at the
     group's values, because two rules that agree today are two rules that
     disagree the first time one of them is edited.

     THE MONO WENT WITH THEM, AND THAT IS THE SUBSTANCE OF THE CHANGE. This row
     was set in the face the site gives to devices — "an instruction to a device"
     — which was right while it stood alone between a subscribe field and a
     governance row. It now stands third in a set of three lines the editors
     asked to read as matched, and a monospace line among two serif ones is the
     one thing that would stop them matching.

     WHAT REMAINS HERE IS WHAT IS THIS ROW'S ALONE: the mini, the vanishing act
     when the journal is already installed, its place in the group's rhythm, and
     its own centring. */
  .footer-app {
    margin: 0.55rem 0 0;
    /*
      CENTRED HERE RATHER THAN INHERITED, and the reason is scoping. Base.astro
      centres the footer with `footer p { text-align: center }`, which Astro
      compiles with ITS OWN component id on both selectors — so it reaches
      every <p> the layout writes and reaches none that a component writes.
      This row is a component, so it arrived left-aligned in a stack where
      everything else is centred.

      Restating the rule is the fix rather than reaching across the boundary
      (`:global(footer p)` from here, or a `.footer-app` case added to
      Base.astro): a component that can only be laid out correctly by the
      layout that happens to hold it is a component that breaks the moment it
      moves. The row carries its own alignment.

      THE UNIT IS THE ICON AND THE WORDS TOGETHER. The anchor below is
      inline-flex, so it shrinks to the width of its contents and this rule
      centres that whole box — mini and text as one thing, not a centred line
      of text with a picture hanging off its left.
    */
    text-align: center;
  }

  /* The colour is the group's, reaching this link through `a { color: inherit }`
     in global.css — so there is no green declared here to fall out of step with
     the two lines above. */
  .footer-app a {
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 0.5rem;
    text-decoration: none;
    /* The letterspacing above puts a trailing space after the final letter,
       which is real width and pulls the centred unit a hair to the left. Taken
       off the right so the optical centre is the geometric one. */
    margin-right: -0.14em;
  }

  .footer-app a:hover span,
  .footer-app a:focus-visible span {
    text-decoration: underline;
    text-decoration-color: currentColor;
  }

  /* The mini is the tile at the height of the words beside it, rounded the way a
     phone rounds it — the smallest place on the site the app's own picture
     appears, so a reader recognises what they installed when they see it on
     their home screen.

     SIZED IN `em`, NOT IN PIXELS (editors, 2026-09-07). It was a fixed 18px
     against 12.24px type; the line is 15.3px type now, and an icon that does not
     track the text is an icon that has to be re-measured every time the group's
     size moves. 1em is the type's own size, which is what "matched to the text"
     means and what keeps the three lines reading as one set. The width and
     height attributes on the tag are the fallback box before CSS lands and are
     kept close to the rendered size so nothing jumps.

     The tile is full bleed and square, so one value does both axes. */
  .footer-app-icon {
    border-radius: 3px;
    width: 1em;
    height: 1em;
    display: block;
  }
</style>
